---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Naming Conventions"
  description="How STACSS approaches CSS selectors and class names."
>
  <header>
    <h1 class="gradient-text">Naming Conventions</h1>
    <p>
      How STACSS approaches CSS selectors and class names.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <h2>Prefer simple selectors</h2>
    <p>
      STACSS favors simple selectors and modern HTML structures.
      Avoid overly verbose naming systems whenever possible.
    </p>
    <p>
      Modern HTML semantics — semantic elements, state attributes, and natural nesting —
      often eliminate the need for elaborate naming conventions.
    </p>

    <div class="naming-compare">
      <div class="naming-block less">
        <div class="label">BEM <span class="note">— verbose</span></div>
        <div class="part">
          <span class="lang">HTML</span>
          <pre><code>&lt;nav class="nav"&gt;
  &lt;a class="nav__item--is-active"&gt;</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="part">
          <span class="lang">CSS</span>
          <pre><code>.nav__item--is-active</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="naming-block preferred">
        <div class="label">Preferred</div>
        <div class="part">
          <span class="lang">HTML</span>
          <pre><code>&lt;nav class="nav"&gt;
  &lt;a class="active"&gt;</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="part">
          <span class="lang">CSS</span>
          <pre><code>.nav a.active</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      Keep selector depth shallow. A selector like <code>.nav a.active</code> communicates
      both structure and state without requiring a naming system. Reserve longer class names
      for cases where context genuinely cannot be inferred from the DOM.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <h2>Component variants</h2>
    <p>
      For component variants, prefer a single flat class name over BEM modifiers.
      Use <code>@extend</code> in Sass so the variant inherits base styles automatically —
      no need to put two classes on the HTML element.
    </p>

    <div class="naming-compare">
      <div class="naming-block less">
        <div class="label">BEM <span class="note">— two classes in HTML</span></div>
        <div class="part">
          <span class="lang">HTML</span>
          <pre><code>&lt;a class="btn btn--primary"&gt;</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="part">
          <span class="lang">Sass</span>
          <pre><code>.btn
  // base styles
.btn--primary
  // variant styles</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="naming-block preferred">
        <div class="label">Preferred <span class="note">— one class in HTML</span></div>
        <div class="part">
          <span class="lang">HTML</span>
          <pre><code>&lt;a class="btn-primary"&gt;</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="part">
          <span class="lang">Sass</span>
          <pre><code>.btn
  // base styles
  &-primary
    @extend .btn
    // variant styles</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      <code>&-primary</code> nested inside <code>.btn</code> makes the relationship
      between base and variant explicit in the stylesheet. <code>@extend .btn</code>
      ensures the variant inherits every base rule — no duplication, no extra class in HTML.
    </p>
  </section>

</DocsLayout>
